          "source_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "canonical_heading": "OVERVIEW",
          "content_blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Anaerobic bacteria require low oxygen tension to live, and constitute a large portion of the normal flora.",
              "pdf_page": 158,
              "printed_page": 76,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "May be Gram-positive or Gram-negative cocci or rods, and individual organisms vary in potential to withstand oxygen exposure.",
              "pdf_page": 158,
              "printed_page": 76,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most common genera—Bacteroides, Fusobacterium,Actinomyces,Propionibacterium, Peptostreptococcus(entericStreptococcus), Porphyromonas, andClostridium.",
              "pdf_page": 158,
              "printed_page": 76,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Most anaerobic infections are polymicrobial and can contain anaerobes admixed with facultative anaerobes or aerobic bacteria (especiallyE. coli).",
              "pdf_page": 158,
              "printed_page": 76,
              "column": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "bullet",
              "level": 0,
              "text": "Injurious toxins and enzymes elaborated by the organisms may allow extension of infection into adjacent, healthy tissue.",
              "pdf_page": 158,
              "printed_page": 76,
              "column": 1
            }
          ]
        },
